  - q: "How long does a cured in place pipe installation take?"
    a: "A residential sewer lateral is almost always a one-day job. Set-up and cleaning take the morning, the liner goes in around midday, the cure runs one to three hours depending on the method, and reinstatement and the final camera pass fill the afternoon. Municipal mains lined manhole to manhole follow the same sequence over one or two shifts per segment."
  - q: "Can I use water during the installation?"
    a: "No. From the moment cleaning starts until the final inspection is done, nothing should go down the drains. Crews usually ask that you not run water from early morning to late afternoon. If the line serves more than one unit, every unit has to hold off."
  - q: "Do they have to dig at all?"
    a: "Usually not. The liner enters through a cleanout, a roof vent, a removed toilet or a manhole. If the line has no usable access point, the crew may dig one small pit to install a cleanout, which then becomes a permanent access for future maintenance. That is a fraction of a trench."
  - q: "What is the smell during curing?"
    a: "Polyester and vinyl ester resins contain styrene, which has a sharp, sweet odor that can drift out of vents and open fixtures while the liner is hot. Crews cap fixtures and vent the exhaust away from the house. Epoxy resins and UV-cured liners produce much less odor. The smell fades once the liner has cooled."
  - q: "What is lateral reinstatement?"
    a: "When a liner is installed in a pipe with branch connections, it covers each one. Reinstatement is reopening those connections from inside with a remote-controlled cutter that the operator steers by camera. Specifications call for the opening to be 90 to 100 percent of the branch diameter with smooth, concentric edges."
  - q: "How do I know the cure worked?"
    a: "Two ways. First, the crew tracks cure temperature and time against the resin manufacturer's schedule, and a good contractor will show you that log. Second, the final camera recording shows a hard, smooth, wrinkle-free liner with every branch reopened. On larger jobs a sample of the cured liner is sent to a lab for bending tests."
