<p class="cl-lead">A cured in place pipe installation looks uneventful from the sidewalk: a truck, a few hoses running into a cleanout, a crew watching monitors. Inside the pipe, a new structure is being formed to the shape of the old one. Here is what happens at each step, roughly how long it takes on a typical house sewer, and the points where good crews separate from the rest.</p>

<p>The sequence below is the one written into ASTM F1216 (inversion) and F1743 (pulled-in-place) and into the sewer specifications of cities across the country. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe">cured in place pipe</a> overview covers the method in general; this page is the day itself.</p>

<ol class="cl-steps">
  <li><b>Camera inspection and locating</b>
  A push camera or crawler records the full length of the pipe. The operator notes material, diameter, every branch connection and its clock position, offsets, cracks, roots and any sag that holds water. A locator on the surface marks where each defect sits so the crew knows what it is lining through. Public specifications require this recording to be made no more than 24 hours before lining, because a root mass can regrow or a joint can shift. On a house sewer this takes 30 to 60 minutes.</li>

  <li><b>Cleaning</b>
  The pipe has to be clean for the resin to bond and for the liner to sit flush. A hydro-jetter with rotating nozzles strips out grease, scale and sediment. Roots get a cutting head or chain flail. Cast iron with heavy tuberculation gets descaled, sometimes with several passes. Protruding joints or offset lips are ground down by a robotic cutter so they do not become a ridge under the liner. Debris is flushed to a downstream point and removed. Cleaning is where most of the morning goes and where a cheap job gets rushed; a liner over scale is a liner that fails early.</li>

  <li><b>Post-cleaning camera pass</b>
  A second recording confirms the pipe is clean, measures the exact length to the connection points and double-checks each branch location. This recording is the baseline the liner is designed against.</li>

  <li><b>Measuring and wet-out</b>
  The liner tube is cut to the measured length plus a tail at each end. Resin is mixed with its catalyst and worked through the tube so the felt or glass is saturated with no dry spots and no excess. Big crews do this on a wet-out table with a vacuum pulling air out of the tube while rollers set the thickness; UV glass liners arrive pre-impregnated from the factory in light-proof packaging and are kept cool in transit. The liner's design thickness, usually a few millimeters for a residential lateral and more for mains, comes from the F1216 design equations that treat the host pipe as fully deteriorated.</li>

  <li><b>Installation: inversion or pull-in</b>
  <em>Inversion</em> turns the tube inside out into the pipe. The open end is attached to a drum or a standpipe, and water or compressed air pushes the tube forward so it unrolls along the pipe with the resin side pressed against the wall. <em>Pull-in-place</em> drags the wet tube to position with a winch cable from the far access point, then a calibration bladder is inverted inside it and inflated to press it outward. Inversion handles bends and long runs well; pull-in is common for short laterals, point repairs and small diameters where the tube must be positioned precisely between two access points. Either way, pressure is held for the entire cure.</li>

  <li><b>Curing</b>
  The resin hardens on a time-and-temperature schedule set by its manufacturer. Three methods are in use. <em>Hot water</em> is circulated through the inflated liner from a boiler, with sensors at the inlet, the outlet and a probe tucked between liner and host pipe at the far end. <em>Steam</em> does the same job faster and with less water to dispose of. <em>Ultraviolet light</em> is used with glass-reinforced liners under ASTM F2019: a light train is pulled through the inflated liner at a controlled speed and the resin cures as it passes. Hot water on a house lateral runs two to three hours; steam and UV are usually under an hour. The cure is not finished when the heat stops. Specifications require a controlled cool-down, with the water column kept full while cool water is added until the liner is below 100 degrees F, so the new pipe does not shrink or pull away as it cools.</li>

  <li><b>Trimming the ends</b>
  The excess tail at each access point is cut flush and the edge is sealed so flow cannot get behind the liner. On mains, a hydrophilic gasket or a stainless end seal is often installed at the manhole.</li>

  <li><b>Reinstating the branch connections</b>
  Every branch the liner covered is reopened from inside with a remote-controlled cutter guided by camera. The operator finds each connection from the pre-lining recording, cuts a pilot hole, then opens it to 90 to 100 percent of the branch diameter with smooth, concentric edges. On a house sewer this is often one or two connections; on a main it can be dozens. Where a cutter cannot reach, a small excavation and a cored opening is the fallback, which is why the pre-lining locating matters.</li>

  <li><b>Final inspection and documentation</b>
  A last camera pass records the finished liner end to end: smooth, hard, no wrinkles or fins, every connection open, flow moving. You should receive this recording along with the pre-lining recording. On public work the contractor also submits cure logs and, when required, laboratory results from a sample cut from the liner end; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/liner-testing">cured in place pipe liner testing</a> page explains those tests.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What a homeowner actually experiences</h2>
<p>The crew arrives in the morning and asks that no water be used until they say so. Hoses run from the truck to a cleanout in the yard or, if there is none, through a roof vent or a pulled toilet. The jetter is loud for an hour or two. After the liner goes in there is a long quiet stretch while it cures, then the cutter and the final camera. In the late afternoon you get the all-clear and a copy of the before-and-after video. The grass is where you left it. Several of the <a href="/all-contractors">verified installers</a> on this site post completed projects with the day's timeline, such as a <a href="/contractor/tsr-trenchless/projects/san-juan-capistrano-76-ft-sewer-lined">76-foot sewer lined in under six hours while the owner was at work</a>.</p>

<h2>Where installations go wrong</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lining over an unrepaired defect.</strong> A belly stays a belly, and a collapsed section cannot be lined through. Those spots need a <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/repair">cured in place pipe repair</a> plan with a point excavation first.</li>
  <li><strong>Under-cleaning.</strong> Scale or grease under the liner leaves voids and weak bonding at the ends.</li>
  <li><strong>Short cure or fast cool-down.</strong> An under-cured liner stays soft; a liner that cools too fast can shrink and leave an annular gap that lets groundwater track along the outside.</li>
  <li><strong>Missed or ragged reinstatements.</strong> A covered branch backs up the fixture it serves; a ragged cut catches debris and roots.</li>
  <li><strong>Wrong liner for the pipe.</strong> Pressure lines, large diameters and pipes with multiple size changes each need a liner designed for them, which is part of what the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page tells you to ask about.</li>
</ul>
<p>The gear that makes each of these steps possible, from the inversion drum to the UV light train, is covered on the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/equipment">cured in place pipe equipment</a> page.</p>
